{"id":"d5c1805d-dd38-4f71-b04b-755b37808397","object":"chat.completion","created":1793332502,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Locating exact insertion sites before editing.\n\n```python\nfor path, q in [(\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\", \"logger.info\"), (\"deepinfer/logger.py\", \"NewLineFormatter(\"), (\"deepinfer/environ.py\", \"-- Logging --\")]:\n    r = run_command([\"python\", \"read_source.py\", \"--file\", path, \"--search\", q, \"--lines\", 8], cwd=\".\")\n    print(path, r[\"rc\"]); print(r[\"stdout\"][:4000]); print(\"=====\")\n```","reasoning_content":"Now I have full mechanisms. I need to design.\n\nNote environ.py new config: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none()` — but validation >=32 required with error containing variable name. The descriptor wraps errors with f\"{self.name}: {e}\" — so ValueError message includes name. But int_or_none with empty string: `int(\"\")` raises ValueError. Good. But we need >= 32 check. Add factory method `int_or_none_min(min_value)` or a custom init. Task says \"描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法\". I'll add a static factory `int_or_none_at_least(min_value: int)`.\n\nNote empty string raises ValueError from int(\"\") — message \"invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\" then wrapped with name → fine.\n\nAlso the EnvVar.__get__ wraps exception via `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. For ValueError that's fine.\n\nNow logger.py:\n\nNewLineFormatter.__init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None). Validate: if not None: must be int (not bool) and >=32 else ValueError.\n\nformat(): We need to truncate the message body before prefix construction. Approach: build message by doing getMessage on record — but must not mutate record. logging.Formatter.format calls record.getMessage() which sets record.message. Actually Formatter.format does:\n\n```\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\n```\nwhich mutates record by setting `.message` attribute — that's the standard, and stats full handler may recompute? Actually record.message is set to full message; truncation only affects formatted output, so \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\". Setting record.message is standard behavior, fine.\n\nBetter implement format:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) > max_chars:\n            truncated = _truncate_message(message, max_chars)\n            record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)  # copy\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = None\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nCareful: copying record with makeLogRecord(record.__dict__) — makeLogRecord creates a LogRecord with dict; it sets __dict__ = dict. But the copy shares exc_info, stack_info. super().format on the copy will use record.message? Formatter.format computes message = record.getMessage() → record.msg (truncated) with no args → truncated. Then it appends exception text. Good. But note that `record.message` may have been set on the original by another handler; copy's getMessage uses msg. Fine.\n\nBut careful: makeLogRecord requires keys \"name\", \"levelno\", etc. and sets `msg` etc. from dict. makeLogRecord:\n\n```\ndef makeLogRecord(dict):\n    rv = _logRecordFactory(None, None, \"\", 0, \"\", (), None, None)\n    rv.__dict__.update(dict)\n    return rv\n```\nYes fine. But `exc_info` attribute may be set — copying fine.\n\nAlternatively simpler: manually build. But the truncation must still apply multi-line prefix alignment based on record.message. The existing code uses `record.message` after super().format; with the copy, record.message is the truncated message. In the original code, `msg.find(record.message)` — if record.message isn't in msg... e.g. truncated message is in msg (msg ends with message). Works.\n\nHowever there's a subtlety: if exception text present, record.message prefix still at position after prefix. Fine.\n\nBut careful with color: The color wrapping is applied after.\n\nAlso important: if the copy is not used and record is the copied one, we should still use the original record for `record.levelno` etc. That's same values.\n\nAlternative approach avoiding copy: temporarily compute using record.getMessage and build via self._style? Simpler approach:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    if max_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        return self._format_record(record)\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return self._format_record(record)\n    copy_record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n    copy_record.msg = _truncate(message, max_chars)\n    copy_record.args = None\n    return self._format_record(copy_record)\n```\n\nBut wait: if record.message was already set, copy_record.message = full old message, but getMessage() returns msg % args only if args else str(msg). Actually getMessage:\n\n```\nmsg = str(self.msg)\nif self.args:\n    msg = msg % self.args\nreturn msg\n```\nSo copy_record.message gets overwritten in format by `record.message = record.getMessage()`. Good.\n\nHmm, but the copy has `exc_text` maybe cached from previous handler — Formatter.format checks `if record.exc_info: if not record.exc_text: record.exc_text = ...`. If a previous handler already formatted with exc_text computed, copy uses the cached text — that's fine and includes full traceback.\n\nNow `_truncate`: marker = \"...<omitted>...\", len 15. remaining = max_chars - 15. head = (remaining + 1) // 2, tail = remaining - head. Return message[:head] + marker + message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else message[:head]+marker.\n\nCheck max_chars=32: remaining=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓ matches example.\n\nNow default init:\n\n```\nfmt = NewLineFormatter(_FORMAT, datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT, use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(), max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS)\n```\n\nNeed import from deepinfer.environ import envs — check for circular imports: environ.py imports only stdlib. logger imports environ — fine.\n\nNote: validation in formatter for max_message_chars — config already validated >=32.\n\nCareful about `_setup_logger` being called at import: reading env var at import time caches it. Test flow: read 32, set env 64, reinit logger → uses cached 32; then rearm → 64. Since setup runs at import, first access reads env at import. Hmm — but tests may set env var before importing? The task says the test sets it. There's a risk: import of module logger triggers read and caches. Then test sets env var... but they said \"先读取 32，再把环境变量改成 64，重新初始化日志仍使用缓存的32\" implying first access happens explicitly. If the test sets env before importing logger module, fine. Our design follows requirement: read config at init.\n\nBut there is a subtlety: If module is imported before env set, then a test that sets env then calls _setup_logger expects 64? The requirement says cached value; that's their semantics. Follow spec.\n\nAlso \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code handles.\n\nNow stats_logger: change `logger.info(m)`. But note stats_logger's logger is `init_logger(__name__)` = \"deepinfer.engine.stats_logger\" → propagates to root with default handler. Add extra:\n\n```\nlogger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})\n```\nBut nested records... fine. Careful: extra keys can't clash with LogRecord attributes; keep_full_message fine.\n\nAlso note the log worker's `time.sleep` uses dist — not our concern.\n\nLet me check stats_logger lines around 116 for exact text and whether there are other logger.info consuming queue. Let's read the file region. Also need to confirm environ.py around logging section for insertion point, plus rearm functions (already have). And logger.py lines 66-110 (have).\n\nI need to